 and if so  what of your what is your conversation  with him been about yeah well we've  talked he pulled me aside a couple of  times throughout even the offseason and  just you know let me know that he  supports me and he's proud of me for  being able to use this platform to speak  you know on all these issues and and and  that somebody you know needs to do it  and so I think you know I felt they're  very comfortable was doing not only the  the protest but also they work off the  field in these particular areas whether  it's going to DC talking on legislators  or and needing permission to go you know  during the week I've been allowed to do  that being active here in the local  community is setting up meetings with  the police they've been very very  helpful with that well in terms of that  Malcolm Jenkins the Eagles joining me  here on the show and qualm Bolden has  been with you and vice versa going to  Capitol Hill on these subjects and he  flat-out retired from the league saying  that he wants to do that and focus on  that without football in his life what  did you think of his retirement with  that statement Malcolm you know I I was  you know surprised that he retired but I  wasn't surprised at how passionate he  was about these issues  you know he's somebody I've been you  know hit him with this whole offseason  and all of the work when it comes you  know social justice and he's had  recently had you know a family member  killed you know by an officer who was  unarmed and and so a lot of these issues  that play you know across our TV screens  doesn't hit close to home it's in his  home and so I know how passionate he is  about using this platform to make change  so I wasn't really surprised is that  something you'd be thinking about doing  someday what Anquan just did it  retired or presenting you know during  this full time I think you know I'm  obviously football is my number one  thing right now and you know I love the  game and still have a lot of good years  to play but but I do think when  whenever that time is done and I'm done  with the game I think I will you know  obviously continue to use my platform  whether it's to my foundation or is  through activism to make sure that you  know I can I can make the world the  place that I wanted to be for my kids  now what are you what do you think of  Jim Browns comments recently that he  supports Colin Kaepernick but not the  way he's been going about it  disrespecting the national anthem in the  flag what do you think about that  Malcolm Jenkins I mean you know  obviously Jim Brown is somebody who's  got a long legacy of activism and it's  obviously a different way of approaching  it  you know his opinions on what's the  right way to demonstrate what's the  wrong way is you know his opinion I  think the biggest thing is guys starting  to use those platforms to not  necessarily draw attention to the  protest in itself we're starting to  really gear towards solutions geared  toward work and obviously Jim Brown was  one of those people who was very very  solution-based very in the community in  the prisons and I think it's time now to  start to move that conversation to the  work that that is actually being done  there are players that are doing a lot  of different things outside of the  protest that I think can benefit  communities in this country well that's  one of the comments from obviously a  goat in the Pro Football Hall of Fame  and then there's LeSean McCoy from the  current ranks of the National Football  League  saying that Kaepernick is not even that  good enough of a player to bring on a  team to invite whatever distraction or  chaos that might come from his  acquisition and I'm wondering is you as  a defender somebody who plays on the  other side of the ball would you agree  with that assessment of Colin Kaepernick  no I wouldn't just because I don't  consider what he's doing or what he did  last year a distraction um you know a  player who raised in my face every week  you know there's other players around  the league that taken the erasers  you know I don't think anybody feels  that they're irreplaceable or that you  know because they are this great of a  player that you know they're immune to  criticism or whatever I think that's a  ludicrous statement and like I said we  were talking about Colin Kaepernick  we're not talking about him being in  trouble or getting arrested we're  talking about him really standing up for  in justices and I think that context  needs to always be attached to it so I  you know those I think those comments  are unfortunate because you start to see  you know other athletes who don't  realize the platforms that they're  sitting on and then begin to criticize  other players who are using theirs well  I guess in a way he was saying that he's  just not even that good enough of a  player and he's an offensive player so I  guess he would know from the X's and O's  there but you your guy would have to  defend him and if you're actually if  Colin Kaepernick is good enough to play  in the NFL  I think he's proved that already and I  mean there's there's plenty of other  quarterbacks that would prove that he  belongs in the league Malcolm Jenkins  joining me here from the Philadelphia  Eagles couple more minutes and in terms  of football help me out with what the  Eagles are gonna look like this year I  mean you got a second-year quarterback  who who performed extremely well last  year you've got a second-year head coach  with now a full season under his under  his resume well what are the  Philadelphia Eagles gonna look like this  year in terms of goals oriented and then  wins losses Malcolm but you know I think  our goals are to win the division and we  feel like we're way more than capable of  doing that obviously we feel really good  about Carson in the second year and a  lot of new pieces without Sean Jeffery  Torrey Smith LeGarrette Blount Nelson  Aguilar is playing really well for us  that o